According to a new report from Intel Market Research, the global Peripheral Vascular Devices market was valued at USD 8.45 billion in 2025 and is projected to reach USD 16.38 billion by 2034, growing at a robust CAGR of 7.6% during the forecast period (2026–2034). This growth is propelled by the rising global prevalence of diabetes and obesity, an aging population, and rapid technological innovation in minimally invasive endovascular therapies.
Peripheral vascular devices are medical instruments designed to diagnose, treat, and manage conditions affecting blood vessels outside the heart and brain, particularly the peripheral arteries and veins. These devices play a critical role in addressing peripheral artery disease (PAD), chronic venous insufficiency, and other vascular disorders by restoring blood flow, reducing blockages, and preventing complications such as limb ischemia or stroke. Key product categories include angioplasty balloons, stents, atherectomy devices, chronic total occlusion (CTO) crossing devices, embolic protection systems, guidewires, catheters, vascular grafts, and inferior vena cava (IVC) filters. Advances in minimally invasive technologies have significantly enhanced efficacy and safety while reducing patient recovery times.
